DESCR
A worn journal lies open on the desk, its pages dense with a timekeeper's notes.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 2
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC journal toller timekeeper log~
The journal is Toller's.  "Thirty years we were friends," an early page reads,
"he and I, two boys who loved the shape of an hour.  Then Elia died, and something
in him died with her, and he ran -- not away, but sideways, into the years
themselves.  I have followed him through age after age of this dreadful place, and
he does not know my face.  To Midian I am only a shadow at his heels, a rival, a
thing to outrun.  He would weep if he knew it was me.  So I do not tell him.  I
only follow, and try to reach the work before it finishes him."  Lower down, in a
steadier hand: "The clock here opens the way -- built in the order the pendulums
are tuned, longest beat to shortest, the last piece the key.  -- for M, whatever
he has become."~
End

#31028
NAME   heat-scarred logbook log book~
SHORT  a heat-scarred logbook~
DESCR
A heat-scarred logbook lies open here, its pages fused at the edges.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC logbook log book~
The logbook's cover is warped and its pages fused at the edges, but the middle
survives.  The hand is Midian's, still firm in these years, still proud.  "Fire
is the first honest power," one entry reads.  "The pendulum and the lens only
watched time; fire feeds it.  I can pour heat into the seam and force it wider."
Later the pride curdles: "The others feel it now.  I have pushed heat into our
hour, and three hundred years downstream a summer never ends.  I did not think
the wound would travel."  A final legible line: "The inner hearth is sealed.
Only the key drawn from the great crucible will open it -- and only fire can make
that key."~
End

#31029
NAME   cinder key crucible-forged crucible forged~
SHORT  a crucible-forged cinder-key~
DESCR
A key of dark iron lies at the crucible's edge, its teeth still glowing a dull red.~
MAT    metal~
TYPE   key 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC key cinder-key cinder crucible~
The key was drawn straight from the great crucible and never allowed to cool;
its teeth are shaped like tongues of flame, and it glows a sullen red that no
forge could sustain for this long.  It is warm -- almost too warm -- to hold,
and the heat of it seems to lean toward the lock it was made for.~
End

#31030
NAME   cinder cold burns final work piece~
SHORT  a cinder that burns cold~
DESCR
A single ember rests upon the pedestal here, glowing, though the air around it aches with cold.~
MAT    mineral~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC cinder cold ember piece~
It looks like a live coal, small enough to close your hand around, and it sheds
a steady orange light -- yet it draws warmth out of everything near it, so that
your fingers ache as though you gripped a fistful of winter.  This is a piece of
Midian's final work: a cinder that burns cold, heat and its absence bound into
one impossible thing.  One face of it is ground flat and true, sized and shaped
to be set into a small silver box.~
End

#31031
NAME   console plate cracked message~
SHORT  a cracked console plate~
DESCR
A cracked console plate lies here, still flickering with a half-formed message.~
MAT    metal~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 2
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC plate console message~
Power still crawls through the plate's fractured glass, spelling out the same
unfinished line again and again: "HEAT SEAM STABLE AT -- HEAT SEAM STABLE AT --".
The number never comes.  Whatever reading Midian was waiting for, the instrument
burned out before it could be spoken, frozen forever on the edge of an answer.~
End

#31032
NAME   heat-sink rod heatsink sink rod~
SHORT  a heat-sink rod~
DESCR
A slender rod of frost-blackened metal lies racked against the wall here.~
MAT    metal~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 3
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC rod heat-sink heatsink~
The rod is one of a bank built to drink the wing's excess fire and carry it away
into the cold-vault.  This one is icy to the touch and beaded with frost that
will not melt even in the surrounding heat.  Fine frost-script, etched by no tool
you recognise, spirals along its length -- a warning, perhaps, or an apology.~
End

#31033
NAME   tablet ash-etched warden stone~
SHORT  an ash-etched tablet~
DESCR
A flat stone tablet, its face etched through the soot, leans against the crucible's rim.~
MAT    stone~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 4
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC tablet stone warden~
Someone -- not Midian; the hand is plainer, a servant's or a guard's -- scratched
these words through the soot on the stone: "The master forges his keys in the
fire and forgets them here when the burning takes his mind.  If you mean to open
the sealed hearth, take the glowing key from the crucible's edge.  Do not wait
for it to cool.  It never does."~
End

#31034
NAME   note card logic engine primer problem~
SHORT  a logic-engine primer~
DESCR
A brittle card of waxed paper, printed with gate-symbols and a hand-inked problem, lies here.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC note card logic engine primer problem~
The card is Midian's own primer for the engine that fills these halls.  "Three
gates," it reads, in a hand grown harder and more exact than the young star-
watcher's.  "AND yields ONE only when both feeds are ONE.  OR yields ONE when
either is.  NOT flips what it is given.  From these three I have built an ADDER,
and from the adder, a key."  Below is a single worked problem: "Add, in the old
base of two:  0101 + 0011.  The engine will not open until it reads the answer."
A note in the margin adds: "First seat a charged capacitor in the relay door to
the north, or the gallery stays sealed.  Carry a live bit and a dead bit to the
Adder Console within; it will fuse them into the circuit key."~
End

#31035
NAME   note schematic adder console~
SHORT  the Adder's schematic~
DESCR
A folded schematic, pinned open beside the Adder Console, is covered in careful sums.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC note schematic adder console sum~
The schematic walks the sum out one column at a time, right to left, the way the
engine reads it:  0101 plus 0011.  "One and one make zero, carry one.  Zero and
one and the carry make zero, carry one.  One and zero and the carry make zero,
carry one.  Zero and zero and the carry make one."  At the foot, circled twice:
the answer word, 1000.  "Seat the ONE, then the ZERO, in the order the answer
names them," Midian has written, "and the console will close the circuit and cast
the key.  Precision, now.  The stars forgave a wrong guess.  The current will not."~
End

#31036
NAME   bolt lightning glass frozen piece~
SHORT  a bolt held frozen in glass~
DESCR
A shard of thick glass hangs in the air here, and caught inside it a fork of lightning is frozen mid-strike.~
MAT    mineral~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC bolt lightning glass frozen piece work~
Sealed in a palm of flawless glass is a single bolt of lightning, stopped in the
instant of its leap -- every branch and filament held perfectly still, still
white-hot, and utterly silent.  This is a piece of Midian's "final work," a
moment of raw power measured and pinned so exactly that time itself lets go of
it.  Its base is cut to a small, familiar shape: made, you understand, to be set
into the seat of a silver box.~
End

#31037
NAME   capacitor cell charged component~
SHORT  a charged capacitor cell~
DESCR
A glass capacitor cell, still holding a faint blue charge, sits in a cradle of copper here.~
MAT    metal~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 2
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC capacitor cell charged component~
A hand's-length cylinder of wound foil and clouded glass, warm to the touch and
humming with the last of a charge laid down long ago.  Most of its siblings in
the banks are cracked and dead; this one still holds.  Its copper feet are shaped
to drop into the empty cradle at the heart of the sealed relay door -- the one
thing that will wake the lock and let the gallery doors part.~
End

#31038
NAME   bit token one live charged~
SHORT  a live bit-token~
DESCR
A small brass token stamped with a single upright stroke lies here, crackling faintly.~
MAT    metal~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC bit token one live charged stroke~
A brass disc no wider than a coin, stamped with a single upright stroke -- the
engine's mark for ONE.  It is live: a thin, angry charge runs its rim and stings
the fingers.  The Adder Console will want it, the primer said, seated in its turn
to spell out the answer word.~
End

#31039
NAME   bit token zero dead ring~
SHORT  a dead bit-token~
DESCR
A small brass token stamped with an open ring lies here, cold and inert.~
MAT    metal~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC bit token zero dead ring~
A brass disc stamped with an open ring -- the engine's mark for ZERO.  Unlike its
live twin it holds no charge at all; it is cold, dead metal, and that deadness is
the whole of its meaning.  The Adder Console reads absence as surely as it reads
current.~
End

#31040
NAME   key circuit assembled cast~
SHORT  the assembled circuit key~
DESCR
A key of etched copper and glass, freshly cast by the Adder Console, rests in its tray.~
MAT    metal~
TYPE   key 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC key circuit assembled cast~
The circuit key is a slender wafer of etched copper sealed under glass, its face
a maze of fused logic-lines that still glow a dim, satisfied blue -- the engine's
answer, made solid.  It is cut to fit the great blast door of the experiment
chamber, the only lock left between you and the bolt held frozen beyond it.~
End

#31041
NAME   readout log console paradox~
SHORT  a paradox-engine readout~
DESCR
A brass readout torn from a console lies here, its printed ribbon spooling onto the floor.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC readout log console paradox ribbon~
The ribbon prints the same six lines forever, each stamped a second later than
the last.  "Trial 411: closed the loop by hand.  Held four minutes.  Subject
survived.  Trial 412: closed the loop by hand.  Held four minutes.  Subject
survived."  In the margin, in a shaking version of Midian's neat hand: "A
paradox is only a door with the same room on both sides.  If I can make one on
purpose, I can find the seam in the one that took her -- and reach back through.
I will risk anything now.  There is nothing left that I am afraid to lose."~
End

#31042
NAME   note page corin apprentice~
SHORT  Corin's last page~
DESCR
A single page in a cramped, ink-stained hand has been pressed flat beneath a paperweight here.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC note page corin apprentice~
"He has not been the same since the mistress died.  He does not say her name.
He only works, and the work has gone somewhere I cannot follow him.  Today he
asked me to stand inside the loop while he closed it -- just to hold the moment
steady, he said, only for a minute.  I trust him.  I have always trusted him.
If you are reading this and I have not come out, then the minute did not end,
and it is not his fault, and please -- tell him it is not his fault."~
End

#31043
NAME   shard mirror glass~
SHORT  a cracked mirror-shard~
DESCR
A long shard of mirror lies on the floor, catching the light at a wrong angle.~
MAT    mineral~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC shard mirror glass~
When you lift the shard your reflection arrives a heartbeat behind your hand,
so that for an instant the glass holds an empty room and then, reluctantly,
remembers you were in it.~
End

#31044
NAME   hourglass glass broken~
SHORT  a broken hourglass~
DESCR
A small hourglass lies on its side here, its frame cracked, its sand misbehaving.~
MAT    wood~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 2
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC hourglass glass sand broken~
The sand runs down into the lower bulb, waits, and then climbs back up again,
tracing the same fall over and over.  It has been doing this, you suspect, for
a very long time, and will not stop for you.~
End

#31046
NAME   heartbeat instant moment stilled key~
SHORT  a stilled heartbeat~
DESCR
A single stilled heartbeat hangs in the air here, cold and heavy as a key of ice.~
MAT    mineral~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC heartbeat instant moment stilled~
This is the exact instant of Corin's death, lifted whole out of the loop that
would not let it finish.  Now that it is complete it has weight, and edges, and
a shape -- the blunt, deliberate shape of a key.  It is faintly warm in the
centre and unbearably cold at its rim, and holding it feels like holding still
the only thing in this place that was ever allowed to end.~
End

#31047
NAME   moment piece work paradox unending~
SHORT  a moment that will not end~
DESCR
A sliver of frozen time rests here, no larger than a coin, humming with a sound just under hearing.~
MAT    silver~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC moment piece work paradox unending~
A moment that will not end, sealed by Midian at the height of his madness -- a
single instant made to repeat forever, so that nothing inside it can ever be
lost, because nothing inside it can ever finish.  It is a piece of his final
work, ground and faceted like a gem, and its edge is cut to be set into a small
silver box.  When you hold it to your ear it whispers the same half-second of a
woman's laugh, again, and again, and again.~
End

#31048
NAME   piece certainty final work last~
SHORT  the last certainty~
DESCR
The last certainty rests here on its plinth, impossibly still.~
MAT    silver~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC certainty piece final work last~
No larger than a coin and far heavier than it has any right to be, this is the
seventh and last piece of Midian's final work.  Where the earlier pieces hummed
with borrowed time, this one is simply, flatly certain -- a fixed point you
could no more argue with than argue with the past.  Its underside is cut to
seat into a small silver box, the last of seven sockets it was always meant to
fill.  Set together, the seven were to make the box into the one thing that
could mend a paradox: a fixer of what cannot be fixed.  That joining is for
later.  For now it waits in your hand, quiet and inevitable.~
End

#31049
NAME   page folded note prophecy~
SHORT  a folded page~
DESCR
A single folded page rests on the arm of the chair.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC page note prophecy folded~
The engine still shows me three futures, and two of them are kindnesses it
invents to spare me.  In one she burns; in one she drowns; and in both I am
there, too slow, and the seam tears wider for my trying.  Do not trust the
moving plates.  The true future is the still one -- the empty chair, the seam
sewn shut, Elia breathing, and my own place at the table swept clean of me.
Take the still plate from the orrery.  It is the only key cut to the last door,
and the only end I can bear to make true.~
End

#31050
NAME   plate still fixed future glass~
SHORT  the still prediction-plate~
DESCR
A small pane of glass lies here, showing a moment perfectly still.~
MAT    mineral~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC plate still fixed future glass~
The pane is cool and unmoving in your hand.  Where the other predictions seethe
with fire and water, this one holds a single quiet scene: a plain room, an
empty chair drawn back from a table, a seam in the air stitched closed, and a
woman standing in a doorway, alive and unaware.  Of Midian there is no sign at
all -- not dead, simply never there.  It is the future he dreads and the only
one he will allow.  The plate's edge is cut, oddly, like a key.~
End

#31051
NAME   plate burning fire future glass~
SHORT  the burning prediction-plate~
DESCR
A pane of glass lies here, lit from within by a fire that gives no heat.~
MAT    mineral~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC plate burning fire future glass~
The scene trapped in the glass never stops moving: a cottage takes fire in the
dark, a shape struggles at a window, and a familiar figure reaches the door a
heartbeat too late.  It is vivid, and cruel, and false -- a comfort the engine
offers, a death Midian could tell himself he merely failed to prevent.  Its
edge is smooth and worn.  It fits no lock but grief.~
End

#31052
NAME   plate drowning water future glass~
SHORT  the drowning prediction-plate~
DESCR
A pane of glass lies here, dark and streaming with water that never spills.~
MAT    mineral~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC plate drowning water future glass~
Within the glass a night river rises without end; a pale hand slips beneath the
black water, and a boat strains always one stroke short.  Like the fire, it is
convincing in the way a nightmare is convincing -- all weight, no truth --
another kindly lie in which the fault was only slowness, only luck.  Its edge
is smooth and fits nothing.  It is not the future he chose.~
End

#31053
NAME   letter midian final elia~
SHORT  a letter in a familiar hand~
DESCR
A letter juts from one of the few drawers that remain.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC letter midian final elia~
It is addressed to no one, or to her.  "I have watched every road, Elia," it
begins, "and there is only one where you go on and the world holds.  It is the
road where I am never on it.  I cannot cut the paradox from inside; every
attempt only widens the wound.  The single cut that does not bleed is the one
that takes away the hand that made it -- takes away me, back before the
beginning, so the beginning never comes.  I am not brave.  I am only certain.
Forgive me the arithmetic of it.  I would rather be forgotten than be the
reason you were."~
End

#31054
NAME   lens seer reading crystal~
SHORT  a seer's lens~
DESCR
A seer's lens of ground crystal rests on the reading-desk.~
MAT    mineral~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC lens seer reading crystal~
A disc of ground crystal on a worn brass handle, the tool Midian used to read
the prediction-plates true.  Held up to the light it sorts the honest futures
from the flattering ones: the moving scenes smear and run under its gaze,
revealing themselves as invention, while a true prediction stays sharp and
still.  Only one of the orrery's three plates does not blur when you raise the
lens to it -- the quiet one, the empty chair.~
End

#31055
NAME   note toller last final timekeeper~
SHORT  Toller's last note~
DESCR
A page in a jagged, hurried hand lies beneath a dead man's open hand.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC note toller last final~
The hand is Toller's, the letters burned into the page as much as written: "M.
cannot cut himself free from the inside -- I have watched him try, and every try
tears the wound wider.  But I am not inside it.  I never was.  I am only the
timekeeper who followed him here, the friend he no longer knows.  An outside hand
can close what an inside hand cannot -- and it must be paid for from outside, in a
life freely given.  I have the charge built.  I know what it will do to me.  In
all this chase he never once knew my face, so you must be the one who knows it:
tell it true, that it was Toller, his oldest friend, who paid.  Take what is left
of me onward.  Finish it.  Save them both.  You cannot save me, and that is right
-- someone has to be the price."~
End

#31056
NAME   watch toller pocket ticking friend~
SHORT  Toller's watch~
DESCR
A watch, still wound and softly ticking, is cupped in the finger-bones of a skeleton here.~
MAT    silver~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC watch toller pocket ticking~
Toller's watch, still wound, still ticking, carried to the very end and past it --
a timekeeper's own timepiece, made and wound from outside the paradox, the one
clock Midian could never wind for himself.  This is the thing his whole sacrifice
was folded into.  Carried to the End of Time, an outside hand could use it to
close the wound for good: to give Midian back his life and Elia back her breath,
and undo the trap entire.  All of it, save the one man who made it possible -- the
best friend Midian chased across the years and never once recognized.  The watch
ticks on in your hand, patient as its owner, counting out a time he will never
see, for a man who will never know he had it counted.~
End

#31057
NAME   journal toller second chase page~
SHORT  Toller's travelling journal~
DESCR
A travelling journal, its cover warped by heat, has been set down open here.~
MAT    paper~
TYPE   trash 0 0 0 0 0
EXTRA  0 0
WEAR   A
LEVEL  0
WEIGHT 1
COST   0
COND   100
EDESC journal toller second chase page~
Toller's hand again, later and more desperate: "Another age, another failure to
reach him.  He burns himself for power now, and I stood not ten feet away while he
looked through me and asked, quite politely, that the 'stranger' stop crowding his
instruments.  I laughed, so that I would not weep.  Thirty years, and I am a
stranger crowding his instruments.  I begin to see what saving him will cost --
not merely a hand from outside, but a life paid from outside, one for one.  I am
not afraid of that.  I am afraid only that when it is done he will still never know
it was me."~
End
